## Residuals

Ordered most- to least-serious. Every `confirmed (not fixed — stopped)` entry carries its full description and proposed fix.

### confirmed (not fixed — stopped)

**SR-001 · critical · §7 Dispatch and the fixer · [internal-consistency, contracts]**
§7.1 and §7.2 state incompatible rules about which component blocks reach the fixer. §7.1: "Components that are already fixed or rejected are not included; if that leaves fewer than two, the composite is degenerate (§6.2) and is not dispatched." §7.2 Phase 1: "Every ID in `Composed-of` must have a block in the prompt; a missing one is a **Failed** verdict with an explicit error naming the ID." §12 repeats the second rule as a closed-list row. Because §4.1 makes `Composed-of` the source of truth and §4.3 has it travel in the dispatch copy, the fixer receives all N IDs and only the surviving blocks and cannot tell a deliberately excluded component from a lost one — so a composite dispatched per §7.1 is failed per §7.2. The case is reachable and blessed by §12 ("Component fixed alone via `/fix` while its composite is open | Composite unaffected") and §9 ("extracts the unfixed component blocks"): a three-component composite with two unfixed components is dispatchable, arrives with two blocks against three IDs, and is forced Failed. The same sections also disagree on rejected components: §7.1 excludes them, Phase 1 "skips and lists" them, Phase 6 carries `skipped (rejected)` — a value §7.1 makes unreachable.
*Proposed fix (finder 1):* In §7.2 Phase 1: "Every ID in `Composed-of` that carries no `**Status:**` line must have a block in the prompt"; in §7.1: "Components carrying `✅ Fixed` or `⚠️ Partially Fixed` are not included; a component carrying `🚫 Rejected` travels and Phase 1 skips it (Result `skipped (rejected)`)"; restate the §12 row as "payload missing the block of an unfixed component". *(finder 2):* alternatively add a `**Skipped-components:** <ID> (fixed|rejected), …` line to the §7.1 payload and require Phase 1 to accept an ID either with a block or on that line.

**SR-002 · major · §3 Definitions · [internal-consistency]** *(same defect as SR-009, anchored at the definition)*
§3 defines "bound component" as a component of an open composite, never dispatched on its own, and "degenerate composite" as an *open* composite with fewer than two unfixed components — so a degenerate composite's lone component is bound. §6.2 step 2 then dispatches it alone, which §3 forbids; step 1 removed it from the individual list and nothing returns it; §6.6 excludes bound components from the pre-flight table and `total_count`. One implementer fixes an issue absent from the pre-flight and the gate's count; another drops the finding silently.
*Proposed fix:* Scope binding to *dispatchable* composites in §3, and add to §6.2 step 2 that the lone component is released for the run — it rejoins the individual list and appears in the pre-flight table and `total_count`. Note in §3 that `/fix` on a bound component may still fix it alone per §9.

**SR-006 · major · §15 Acceptance protocol · [ambiguity-testability]**
The protocol's only precondition ("a branch whose review yields at least one composable group") cannot produce four of its checkboxes: (a) a challenger-removed basis; (b) a fix-time proposal — once `/review` binds the composable group, its findings are no longer candidates, so a *second* group the cross-verifier missed is required and nothing requires it; (c) a Partially Fixed composite; (d) a needs-decision composite. §13 makes the protocol a release gate, so two operators reach opposite verdicts on the same run: one ticks vacuously, the other blocks.
*Proposed fix:* Replace the preamble with the full setup (two composable groups, one grouped by the cross-verifier and one not; a component with a non-`auto` `Fix-policy` inside a group; a challenger-removed basis; hand-edited fixture reports where a branch cannot produce one) and a disposition rule: an unexercised item is recorded `N/A — <reason>`, never ticked, and an N/A on the fix-time-persistence, Partially-Fixed or needs-decision items blocks release.

**SR-007 · major · §8 Decision gate · [ambiguity-testability, contracts]**
"Stage 4 grades as today. The write-back propagates statuses to components per §7.3" leaves the source of a component's status underdetermined on the gate path. §7.3's component columns key on the fixer's Components table (Phase 4.4 self-judgment), while stage 4 decides from tree observations and stage 3.5's raw output and states the fixer's verdict is advisory. Reading A writes terminal `✅ Fixed` on components from the fixer's self-report; reading B grades each component from its own plan check, which §8's plan clause guarantees exists. A `✅ Fixed` written from narration the raw signal contradicts freezes that finding out of every later run.
*Proposed fix:* After the Stage 4 bullet: a component's `✅ Fixed` on this path is decided by that component's own check in the decided verification plan, graded on raw output exactly as the finding's own status is; the fixer's Components table is advisory and never decides a status; where the plan carries no attributable check for a component, no component status is written and the composite's status releases it. Rekey §7.3's component columns accordingly and, for the plain `/fix-all` path, label the fixer's `Result` advisory in the Fix Summary. *(The user's decision on SR-022 adopts exactly this split.)*

**SR-008 · major · §5 Review side · [ambiguity-testability, completeness]**
§4.2 states "A component belongs to at most one open composite" and the fix-time pass enforces it (§6.3 check 3, §6.4), but §5.2's per-composite procedure never does: nothing stops the cross-verifier citing one finding as a basis of two composites, and nothing arbitrates what `/review` then does — two `Part-of` values for one block, two open composites binding one finding, which §6.2 would dispatch twice.
*Proposed fix:* Insert into §5.2 between steps 1 and 2: a basis already resolved into an earlier composite (cross-verifier output order) is dropped from every later one; a composite left with fewer than two bases falls to the bullet path. Add the row to §12's closed list.

**SR-009 · major · §6 The composition pass · [ambiguity-testability, completeness]** *(same defect as SR-002, anchored at §6.2/§6.6)*
§6.2 item 2 ("With one remaining component, that component is dispatched alone") conflicts with §3 (the component is still bound, since the composite is still open) and §6.6 (bound components hidden from the table and `total_count`). Reading A dispatches and fixes an issue absent from the pre-flight and the Step 2.5 count, bypassing the severity floor and the Fix-policy partition for it — a sub-floor or needs-decision lone component could be auto-fixed. Reading B returns it to the individual list.
*Proposed fix:* Extend §6.2 item 2: the lone component returns to the individual list, passes the floor and the partition, is rendered as an ordinary row and counted in `total_count`; only the components of a *dispatchable* composite are hidden. Mirror in §6.6: "Bound components of a dispatchable composite do not appear in the table."

**SR-014 · major · §9 `/fix` · [completeness, feasibility]**
`/fix` never dispatches `fix-auto` — `commands/fix.md` has no `Task` grant and runs its own Phases 1–8, offset by one from `fix-auto`'s from Phase 3 on. §7.2 defines composite mode for `fix-auto` only; §9 gives `/fix` Step 0.4 payload assembly, Step 0.4.5 and "Phase 8 propagates statuses per §7.3", but §7.3's mapping consumes per-component results only §7.2's Phase 4.4/Phase 6 produce. §9's "Phase 1's composite-mode error" commits `/fix` to a composite mode of its own. `/fix`'s Phase 1 Category enum (`Security|Performance|Architecture|Maintainability|Documentation|Testing`, fix.md:158) lacks `Composite`, §17 does not list that change, and `check-prefix-sync.sh` greps only `agents/fix-auto.md` for the enum, so it will not catch it. One implementer mirrors §7.2 into `/fix`; another lets the single-issue flow run, patching components locally with no per-component result to propagate.
*Proposed fix:* Add to §9: `/fix COMP-NNN` runs the §7.2 composite mode over its own phases — Phase 1 parses `Composed-of` and the component blocks with the same required-field and missing-block rules and gains `Composite` in its enum; Phase 2 reads every location; Phase 3's proposal names the single change and the components it must resolve; Phase 4 implements the composite Remediation only, under the verbatim rule, including in Phase 6's iterations; Phase 5 uses the union rule plus the per-component re-read; Phase 7 carries the Components table and the three-verdict rule. Extend §17's `commands/fix.md` row and §13.1's invariants accordingly.

**SR-015 · major · §6 The composition pass · [completeness, feasibility]**
`/fix-all` Step 2.2.5's zero-auto path (fix list empty, `needs_decision` non-empty) skips "the rest of Step 2 and the whole of Steps 3–4" and goes straight to Step 5 — bypassing Step 2.4 (pre-flight), Step 2.4.5 (dissolve question) and Step 3.0 (persistence), all of which §6.6–§6.8 place inside it. §6.1 still runs the pass at Step 1.6 over needs-decision findings, so proposals exist, every one needs-decision (§4.1), and none is rendered, vetoed or written — yet §8 sends the composite through the gate, whose stage 2 writes `**Decision:**` and a pin into a block that is not in the report file. Discarding vs. persisting at Step 5.2 are observably different, and only the second preserves §2's veto goal.
*Proposed fix:* Add a clause to §6.7 and §6.8 (and §6.1's placement paragraph): on the zero-auto path, render the Composites block and ask the dissolve question at Step 5.2 before the resolve offer, and persist the surviving composites immediately after "yes" at Step 5.2 and before stage 0 — the same committed-to-dispatching point, relocated; a "no" writes nothing. Add a `/fix-all` zero-auto row to §12.

**SR-016 · major · §4 Report format · [completeness]**
§4.1 says nothing about `**Source:**` / feedback-origin provenance, and §6.1 explicitly groups over feedback reports. A composite over feedback-origin components carries no provenance marker, while §6.2/§6.6/§6.9 hide the bound components from the pre-flight table and the checklist — so the untrusted-provenance signal that `/fix-report` Step 1.4 and `/fix-all`'s `Source` column exist to surface disappears at exactly the human gates where the grouped fix is approved. The gap reaches §8: stage 1's nonce-wrapping keys on a `**Source:**` line, so the component blocks are wrapped but the composite's model-authored `Problem`/`Remediation` — derived from untrusted text — travel unwrapped to an analyst holding an unrestricted shell, and stage 2's `Source` row shows nothing. Not recorded as deferred in §2, §12 or §16.
*Proposed fix:* Add to §4.1: a composite whose `Composed-of` names any component carrying `**Source:**` is itself feedback-origin; it carries `**Source:** @handle1, @handle2 — inherited from <IDs>`, and every consumer that keys on `**Source:**` treats it as a component — the pre-flight `Source` column, Step 1.4's untrusted-provenance block, stage 1's nonce wrapping of its `Problem`/`Impact`/`Remediation`, stage 2's `Source` row. Reference from §6.4 and §8.

**SR-018 · major · §6 The composition pass · [feasibility, doctrine-compliance]**
§6.4 validates the analyst-authored `Location` as "parses … under the two-clause read rule … and the path exists in the working tree" — the usability rule with its containment conjunct dropped. In `decision-gate` the rule has three conjuncts (parses ∧ contained ∧ exists) and says why existence alone is insufficient (`/etc/hosts:1`, `../../.ssh/authorized_keys:1` both parse and exist); the phrasing §6.4 copies is `fix-all` Step 2.4's, a render site that disclaims containment. §6.4 is a validation site; the value is model-authored over candidate blocks that may include feedback text, is persisted as the composite's `**Location:**`, and is dispatched to `fix-auto` with unrestricted `Edit`/`Write`/`Bash`. For an `auto` composite nothing else catches it: stage 0 never runs, and `fix-auto` Phase 1 only parses. §4.1's claimed safety net overstates the auto path.
*Proposed fix:* In §6.4: "`Location` is usable under *The usability rule* in `code-review:decision-gate` — parses by the two-clause read rule, **is contained in the repository tree by that section's three-step containment test**, and exists there; a proposal failing any conjunct is dropped and listed with `location-unusable`." Add the containment requirement to §4.1's `**Location:**` rule and note §6.4 is the only containment gate an `auto` composite passes.

**SR-019 · major · §13 Testing · [feasibility]**
§13 opens "verification is contract checks in CI plus an acceptance protocol", but nothing runs the contract test in CI: §13.2's new workflow runs `check-prefix-sync.sh` only, §17 lists it as the sole new workflow, so `test_composite_contract.py` runs only by hand — the state §13.2 itself calls the defect. The repository's contract workflows each run their test file explicitly; an unreferenced file under `plugins/code-review/tests/` is invisible to all of them. The declared shape is wrong too: §13.1 says "pytest, in the shape of `scripts/test_check_*.py`", but both are stdlib `unittest` modules run as `python3 <file>`, and `execution-boundary.yml` records keeping its checks dependency-free; no workflow installs pytest.
*Proposed fix:* In §13.1: "stdlib `unittest`, run as `python3 plugins/code-review/tests/test_composite_contract.py`, in the shape of `scripts/test_check_*.py`". In §13.2: the new workflow runs both the contract test and `bash plugins/code-review/scripts/check-prefix-sync.sh` on pull requests against `master`; rename it; update §17's workflow row and §15's last checkbox.

**SR-022 · major · §7 Dispatch and the fixer · [doctrine-compliance] · needs-decision — decided B**
Bar item 2. §7.2's Step 4.4 makes the actor its own per-component verifier ("re-read its location … decide whether its Problem still holds; record `resolved`"), §7.3 maps that self-report straight into terminal `✅ Fixed` writes, and Step 1.3 then filters those findings forever. One dispatch's narration closes N findings where today it closes one. On the needs-decision path the spec creates an independent signal (the per-component plan check, run and logged by the orchestrator at stage 3.5) and then discards it: "Stage 4 grades as today. The write-back propagates statuses to components per §7.3" — the component column comes from the fixer's table. `decision-gate` stage 3.5 states the invariant this inverts. §16 discloses that the root-cause-only rule is prose-enforced and that composite verification is a re-read judgment, but not that the judge is the actor being graded.
*Decision (B, recorded above):* gate path keyed to the plan checks on raw output, fixer table advisory; auto path keeps the fixer's verdict with an explicit advisory label in §16 and the Fix Summary.

**SR-023 · major · §6 The composition pass · [doctrine-compliance]**
Bar item 4. §6.7 makes one `AskUserQuestion` "the one human gate on a grouping" and §16 names it the sole guard, but nothing says what happens when the call is unavailable or errors. The failure is fail-open by construction: "(select none to keep all)" makes an empty, errored or absent answer indistinguishable from "keep every composite", after which the run fixes each group as a unit with no veto. §6.5's fail-safe covers the analyst only; §12's closed list has no row for the gate; `decision-gate`'s abort-on-AskUserQuestion-failure rule is scoped to the decision stage, which an all-`auto` composite never enters; `/fix-all` Step 2.5's abort covers only its own gate. No interactivity probe and no headless opt-in exist.
*Proposed fix:* Add to §12: "Dissolve question cannot be asked (`AskUserQuestion` unavailable or errors)" → no composite is dispatched as a unit this run: every proposed composite is dropped (nothing persisted), every persisted composite is treated as dissolved for the run with its components dispatched individually, and the pre-flight and Composition block carry `Composition gate: unavailable — <reason>`. State the fail-closed direction in §6.7 beside "the one human gate", and record in §16 that the design cannot probe interactivity and depends on the ask failing loudly.

**SR-024 · major · §6 The composition pass · [doctrine-compliance]**
Bar item 9. A composite's `Problem` and `Remediation` are newly generated assertions: the analyst authors them and administers its own battery (§6.3); §6.4 validates structure only and never re-runs an `Evidence` citation, unlike `decision-gate`'s reject gate. §16 says the dissolve question is the human gate — but that gate never shows the assertion it guards: §6.7's description carries `<n> components: … — <first sentence of Problem>`, §6.6's block shows ID, origin, title and component IDs, and the `Remediation` — the one structural change applied to the user's source in place of N local fixes — is rendered nowhere and, for a fix-time proposal, is not in the report either (§6.8 persists after the gate). The user vetoes a membership list, not the change.
*Proposed fix:* Render the assertion at the point of veto: add the composite's full `Problem` and `Remediation` to §6.6's Composites block for every dispatchable composite, state in §6.7 that the block is the gate's evidence. Optionally strengthen §6.4 by re-running one cited `Evidence` call per proposal under the read-only boundary and dropping a proposal whose citation does not reproduce.

**SR-027 · major · §6 The composition pass · [ux]**
§6.7 specifies "One `AskUserQuestion`, `multiSelect: true`" then pages it ("`(page X of Y)` appended when more than four composites exist, four options per call") without defining an appended navigation item or saying selections accumulate. `/fix-report` Step 2.2b already worked this out the other way: the appended skip item spends one of the four slots, so a followed page holds 3. "Four options per call" is satisfied by both readings, so two implementers build different gates: ⌈Y/4⌉ forced pages of four with no early exit, or three per page with a skip item that can end the question with composites never displayed — silently keeping a grouping the user was never shown. §6.7 calls this "the one human gate", so both the answer count and full display are load-bearing.
*Proposed fix:* After the paging clause: "The four-option ceiling is spent entirely on composites — nothing is appended, unlike `/fix-report`'s checklist pages. Every page is asked in sequence, selections accumulate across pages, and no page is skippable: the run costs ⌈Y/4⌉ answers and every dispatchable composite appears on some page."

**SR-031 · major · §14 Versioning and documentation · [contracts]**
The compatibility claim carrying the MINOR bump is false as written: "Pre-existing reports parse and dispatch exactly as before." §6.1 runs the pass over every `docs/reviews/` report, feedback reports included — every pre-existing review and feedback report. On such a report, 2.1.0 no longer dispatches one `fix-auto` call per finding, and §6.8 writes new `COMP-NNN` blocks and `Part-of` lines *into* the pre-existing file before the first fixer call. §2's goal "Old reports, QA reports and feedback reports keep working unchanged" is contradicted by the same passage. The claim is the stated justification for MINOR and feeds the Upgrade Notes entry.
*Proposed fix:* Replace with the true claim: pre-existing reports **parse** exactly as before — no field renamed, no block rewritten, every consumer of the old grammar keeps reading it; they do not **dispatch** exactly as before: Step 1.6 runs over any `docs/reviews/` report, so an old report's findings may be grouped into one call and `COMP-NNN` blocks and `Part-of` lines may be written into it; the dissolve question is the veto, and no write happens before the confirmation gate. Mirror in §2 and require the Upgrade Notes entry to state it.

**SR-032 · major · §4 Report format · [contracts]**
§4.1 rests the `**Location:**` requirement on a guarantee that does not exist: "stage 0 of the decision gate and `fix-auto`'s Phase 1 both refuse a block without a usable location." `fix-auto` Phase 1 does not refuse — it asks, from inside a subagent, which `review.md` Step 5.5 already documents as unreachable and patches around by forcing `needs-decision`. Stage 0 covers needs-decision findings only. The only real validation is §6.4's, scoped to fix-time proposals; §5.2's review-origin construction takes `Location` from the cross-verifier with no parse or existence check. An `auto` review-origin composite with an unusable `Location` reaches the fixer's unreachable ask, and the text does not settle where the check belongs.
*Proposed fix:* In §5.2 item 3 add the §6.4 check (parse by the two-clause rule, path exists — and per SR-018, contained); a composite whose `Location` fails is not rendered as a block and stays a bullet. In §4.1 restate the enforcement accurately: required and enforced by the producers — §5.2 at review time, §6.4 at fix time — plus stage 0 for a needs-decision composite; `fix-auto`'s Phase 1 asks rather than refuses, and that ask is unreachable inside a subagent, so no composite may be dispatched unvalidated.

### reported-only (minors and the nit; the sub-major needs-decision entry is never batched)

- **SR-003 · minor · §6 · [internal-consistency, ux]** — The worked delta line `Dissolved COMP-002: +2 issues, 14 total` contradicts §6.6's counting rule (a two-component composite dissolves to +1 in `total_count`); whether one line prints per dissolved composite or one aggregate is undefined; the gate's count then names two issues absent from the on-screen table. *Fix:* one line per dissolved composite naming the released components and the net change — `Dissolved COMP-002 — released PERF-001, PERF-003 (+1 issue); 14 total` — and state that the last line's total is what the gate's question and its `Yes — fix all <total_count>` label use.
- **SR-004 · minor · §12 · [internal-consistency]** — The closed error list omits two situations §6.8 defines: a `Part-of` write that fails (recorded; membership unaffected) and a dissolution write that fails (recorded; treated as dissolved for the run, offered again next run). *Fix:* add both rows.
- **SR-005 · nit · §6 · [internal-consistency]** — "One `AskUserQuestion`" and §15's "appears once" conflict with the paging clause in wording only. *Fix:* "One `AskUserQuestion` per page (a single call for four composites or fewer)"; §15: "appears once per page".
- **SR-010 · minor · §6 · [ambiguity-testability, ux]** — "persisted" has two senses: §6.6's pre-flight `1 proposed, 1 persisted` counts composites already on disk (nothing is written before the gate), §11's `Persisted: 1` counts proposals this run wrote; §6.6 also lacks §11's omission rule, implying an empty `**Composites:**` header on every ordinary run. *Fix:* `**Composition pass:** <N> proposed this run, <M> already in the report`; §11 `Proposed: N | Written: M | Dissolved: K`; omit the block and line when the run holds no composite and the pass was not unavailable.
- **SR-011 · minor · §6 · [ambiguity-testability]** — §6.8 inserts the composite block "immediately before its first component's heading" without defining the order (`Composed-of` order vs earliest in the file); the block may land below some members. *Fix:* "before the heading of whichever component appears earliest in the file, so the block always precedes every member"; mirror in §15.
- **SR-012 · minor · §15 · [ambiguity-testability]** — "persisted before the first dispatch" names an ordering with no observable; the end state looks identical whether written at Step 3.0 or at write-back. *Fix:* "Interrupt the run while the first `fix-auto` call is in flight: the report already carries the composite block, immediately before its earliest-in-file component, with `Part-of` on each component, and a re-run offers the same composite whole."
- **SR-013 · minor · §11 · [ambiguity-testability, ux]** — The Fix Summary row's component markers (`✅`, `—`) are undefined against §7.2's four `Result` values; a user cannot tell whether `ARCH-001 —` survived, was unchecked for lack of a location, or was skipped as rejected. *Fix:* a legend under the Fix Summary — `✅ resolved · ❌ unresolved · ? unresolved (no location) · 🚫 skipped (rejected)` — stating a component's glyph is the `Result` its row carried.
- **SR-017 · minor · §6 · [completeness]** *(same defect as SR-036)* — §6.8 write 2 has no rule for a component already carrying `Part-of` (reachable: a dissolved composite leaves the line in place; the released finding is re-groupable). *Fix:* replace in place; a block carries at most one `**Part-of:**` line; mirror in §4.2.
- **SR-020 · minor · §6 · [feasibility]** — The `composition-analyst` frontmatter mandates the `finding-falsification` battery but declares no `skills:` key and no `Skill` tool; every other bound agent declares `skills: finding-falsification`, and the key is in `PERMITTED_KEYS`. *Fix:* add `skills: finding-falsification` to the frontmatter block and to §17's row.
- **SR-021 · minor · §13 · [feasibility]** — §13.3's rationale is wrong: `check_execution_boundary.py` scans `*/commands/*.md` and `*/skills/*/SKILL.md` only, so no agent ever needs a scope row, and a scope row naming the agent's path would fail the build. *Fix:* state the checker's real scope and that the agent must not be given a row; the grant table is unchanged because the fix commands gain no `Bash(...)` grants.
- **SR-025 · minor · §6 · [doctrine-compliance]** — Bar item 6: no wall-clock budget for the pass or the composite dispatch, an unstated analyst timeout, and `Composed-of` has a floor but no ceiling — one composite may bind every candidate in a file and one dispatch then reads N locations, runs N tool rows and N re-reads under the unchanged 3-iteration cap; paging is unbounded in pages. *Fix:* a membership ceiling K in §4.1 with a matching §6.4 check; a stated analyst timeout in §6.5; a wall-clock budget in §6.1, or a §16 entry accepting its absence.
- **SR-026 · minor · §6 · [doctrine-compliance] · needs-decision (sub-major, never batched)** — Bar item 10: a dissolved proposal is not durable — "nothing is written", the number is released, "may be proposed again next run" — so wherever the released components are not all fixed in the same run (partial `/fix-report` selection, a Failed fix, a sub-floor component), an unchanged report yields the identical proposal and the identical veto is re-asked every run; §16's Cost bullet assumes the case away. *Options:* persist the veto (a `**Not-composed-with:** <IDs>` line on each member, or the composite block written with `🚫 Rejected — dissolved before persistence`) and feed vetoed pairs into §6.3's disjointness input; or accept the churn and record it in §16, dropping the "nothing is left to group" claim. *Decision pending — the user's call.*
- **SR-028 · minor · §6 · [ux]** — The dissolve option label (`COMP-001 (review)`) carries no severity or title, the description names components as bare IDs, and in `/fix-report` (no pre-flight) this is the user's only sight of the grouping; §6.9's checklist description drops the Problem sentence Step 2.2c requires. *Fix:* label `[HIGH] COMP-001: <title>` (60-char rule), description `<n> components: SEC-002 <title>, … — <first sentence of Problem> · <origin>`; keep the Problem sentence in §6.9's item.
- **SR-029 · minor · §6 · [ux]** — §6.9's verbatim checklist description omits the needs-decision prefix Step 2.2c mandates, while the next bullet says "like any other needs-decision finding"; §4.1 forbids `Drift-class` on a composite, so the fallback renders `[needs-decision: —]`. *Fix:* prefix `[needs-decision: via SEC-003]` (or `<ID>+<n> more`), naming the components whose `Fix-policy` forced it.
- **SR-030 · minor · §9 · [ux]** — Step 0.4.5's three options carry no descriptions, unlike every other AskUserQuestion in the plugin; the user typed `/fix SEC-002` and is offered a larger change without its title, severity, co-components or the consequence of the alternative. *Fix:* descriptions — "One root-cause change for COMP-001: <title> (<severity>). Also closes SEC-003, ARCH-001." / "Local fix for this symptom only. COMP-001 stays open and the group returns on the next bulk run." / "Stop now without modifying any files."
- **SR-033 · minor · §6 · [contracts]** — The analyst's return contract closes the terminator's grammar but not the multi-line fields: nothing says where `Problem`/`Remediation` end or that they may not contain a label line, a `### Group` heading, `## Rejected groupings`, or `Composition: `; §6.4 escalates a parse failure to the whole file. *Fix:* "The grammar is closed on its own separators … text that would collide is rewritten or fenced by the agent before it is returned, exactly as `decision-gate`'s `**Verification-plan:**` checks are."
- **SR-034 · minor · §7 · [contracts]** — §7.3's table has columns for `resolved` and `unresolved` only; `unresolved (no location)` and `skipped (rejected)` are settled by §11 (only `resolved` is written to) but not where the mapping is stated. *Fix:* widen the header or add "Only a `resolved` component is written to (§11); the other three `Result` values write nothing; a `skipped (rejected)` component keeps its `🚫 Rejected`."
- **SR-035 · minor · §9 · [contracts]** — `/fix COMP-001` building "the §7.1 payload" makes `/fix` a consumer of stage 3's strip rule, contradicting `decision-gate`'s scope statements ("`/fix` loads it for the `Alternatives:` render format alone"; scope-table row `render-only`); §8 permits a persisted `**Decision:**` on a composite, so an unstripped payload would hand the fixer its own `**Verification-plan:**`. *Fix:* move `fix.md`'s scope row to `dispatch-only` and amend the scope sentence; add to §17's `decision-gate` row and to §9.
- **SR-036 · minor · §4 · [contracts]** *(same defect as SR-017)* — The `**Part-of:**` grammar never bounds the line count and the write recipe never replaces; a released component re-grouped later accumulates a second line. *Fix:* "A block carries at most one `**Part-of:**` line … replaced in place … a consumer meeting more than one reads none and falls back to `Composed-of`."

## Coverage

- Lenses not selected: none — the full seven-lens roster was dispatched.
- Lenses not returned: none.
- Standing blind spots: user intent, external facts, unstated requirements. The panel is one stochastic pass; majors carried no challenger. The batch A fixer and the `fix-coherence` verifier never ran, so no proposed fix was tested for coherence against the whole spec — the proposed fixes above are the reviewers' suggestions, not verified edits.

## Rejected by the panel and the verifier (self-falsification)

### internal-consistency
- [internal-consistency] §4.3 'The new fields are authored fields, not loop-written ones' vs §6.8 having the loop write them — refuted: §4.3's own next sentence reconciles the two ('the composition pass writes its markers before any pin is computed in the same run').
- [internal-consistency] §6.8's /fix-all persistence at Step 3.0 (after the confirmation gate) vs §4.3's 'before any pin is computed' — refuted: /fix-all computes pins in its decision gate at Step 5, after Step 3.0, and /fix-report's Step 2.3.5 precedes its Step 2.4 gate exactly as §6.8 states.
- [internal-consistency] §11 'A composite and each `resolved` component receive a `**Status:**` line' vs §7.3's Failed row ('no status' in all three columns) — refuted: §7.3 is the dedicated status-mapping table and arbitrates; §11 describes the write recipe for the statuses that mapping produces.
- [internal-consistency] §3's 'fewer than two **unfixed** components' vs §12 skipping a component carrying `🚫 Rejected` — refuted: §4.3 treats a block bearing any `**Status:**` line as no longer unfixed, so a rejected component is already excluded from the count.
- [internal-consistency] §2 goal 'never its components separately while the composite is open' vs §9's `/fix SEC-002` alone option — refuted: §9 is an explicit, user-gated exception recorded again in §12; the goal describes automatic dispatch by the fixers (folded into the §3 finding instead).
- [internal-consistency] §14 'Pre-existing reports parse and dispatch exactly as before' and §2 'Old reports, QA reports and feedback reports keep working unchanged' vs §6.1 grouping old review and feedback reports — refuted: the claims are about format compatibility and are qualified by the dissolve veto; grouping is the new feature applied uniformly, not a parse or dispatch break.
- [internal-consistency] §16 'One agent call per report file per run, only while unmarked candidates exist' vs §6.3's 'One call per source file whose candidate set has at least two members' — refuted: the residual-risk line is a cost bound, and §6.3 states the dispatch threshold precisely.
- [internal-consistency] §5.3 'A composite is rendered after the non-composite findings of the same severity' vs §7.1 'No special placement' — refuted: the first governs report rendering order, the second the dispatch queue.
- [internal-consistency] §11's Composition block example 'Dropped by validation: SEC-004 + SEC-005' reusing the pair §6.3 shows under '## Rejected groupings' — refuted: independent illustrations in different sections, not one scenario claimed twice.
- [internal-consistency] §6.3's closing line example 'Composition: 1 groups proposed over 7 findings' — refuted: it matches the fixed vocabulary the spec mandates; grammar of a fixed template is not an inconsistency.
- [internal-consistency] §7.2 Phase 6's verdict rule makes an all-`skipped` composite vacuously 'Fixed' — refuted: §7.1 makes such a payload undispatchable (fewer than two non-skipped components leaves it degenerate).
- [internal-consistency] §15's '/review renders ... a composite whose bases the challenger removed is a bullet, not a block' vs §5.2's threshold of fewer than two surviving bases — refuted: the acceptance line names the case to exercise, §5.2 states the rule; they do not conflict.
- [internal-consistency] §4.3's 'a released component ... is an ordinary unfixed finding **on the next run**' vs §6.7 releasing components within the run on dissolution — refuted: §6.7 states the in-run effect explicitly.
- [internal-consistency] §6.5's 'The pre-flight carries `Composition pass: unavailable — <reason>`' although §6.6 gives /fix-report no pre-flight — refuted: a missing rendering surface for one command is a gap, not a contradiction; §11's Composition block still carries 'Pass unavailable'.
- [internal-consistency] §13.3 'the new agent does not mention `decision-gate`, so it needs no scope-table row' vs §6.3's reference to `code-review:decision-gate` stage 1 wrapping — refuted: §6.3 attributes the wrapping to the dispatching command's prompt, not to text in the agent file.
- [internal-consistency] §7.3's table lacking columns for `unresolved (no location)` and `skipped (rejected)` — refuted: an unmapped enum value is a gap in coverage, not a statement contradicting another statement.
- [internal-consistency] §6.4's 'counting from the highest `COMP-` number the file already carries' reading as either the highest or the highest+1 — refuted: that is ambiguity, owned by another lens.

### ambiguity-testability
- [ambiguity-testability] §7.1 excludes already-fixed components from the payload while §7.2 Phase 1 makes any `Composed-of` ID without a block a Failed verdict — out of mandate: that is a contradiction, owned by internal-consistency.
- [ambiguity-testability] §4.1's `Fix-policy` inheritance when a component carries no `Fix-policy` field — refuted: `/fix-all` Step 2.2.5 states "Absent field ⇒ `auto`", and §4.1 explicitly defers to "the fixers' fail-safe".
- [ambiguity-testability] §7.2 Phase 6 grades a composite whose every component is `skipped (rejected)` as vacuously Fixed — refuted: §7.1's degeneracy filter and §9's `/fix COMP-NNN` extraction keep dispatch from ever producing an all-skipped payload; only a hand-pasted block reaches it.
- [ambiguity-testability] §6.1's "feedback reports included" against the `docs/reviews/`-only rule — refuted: `allocate-feedback-file.sh` writes feedback reports into `docs/reviews/`, so the ID routing holds.
- [ambiguity-testability] §5.2's "Resolve each basis by exact title" when two surviving findings share a title — refuted: the cross-verifier format §5.1 retains labels each basis by domain, scoping the match to one auditor's findings.
- [ambiguity-testability] §6.4's "unparseable" granularity — one malformed `### Group` versus the whole response — refuted: the sentence's subject is "A response", and §12's closed list maps a malformed response to pass-unavailable for that file.
- [ambiguity-testability] §15's "The dissolve question appears once" against §6.7's paging at more than four composites — refuted: §6.7 defines the paging explicitly, so "once" reads as once per run.
- [ambiguity-testability] §3's "open composite" (no `**Status:**` line) applied to a proposed composite that is not yet in any file — refuted: §6.7 and §6.6 name "proposed or persisted" and label proposals explicitly.
- [ambiguity-testability] §6.5's "timeout" names no bound — refuted: the trigger is the platform's own agent-call failure and the handling is identical for all three causes; budget adequacy belongs to another lens.
- [ambiguity-testability] §4.3's "A `Part-of` relation binds while the composite is open" against §4.2's "membership is decided by `Composed-of` alone" — refuted: §4.2 states outright that a missing or dangling `Part-of` changes nothing about what is bound.
- [ambiguity-testability] §4.2's "directly after the heading where the block has no `**ID:**` line" collides with Step 4.1's insert-after-heading `**Status:**` recipe — refuted: placement is cosmetic because no rule reads `Part-of` for membership.
- [ambiguity-testability] §13's grep assertions do not name the strings they grep — refuted: the assertion text is delegated to the named deliverable `test_composite_contract.py`, and the mutation check gives each assertion its pass condition.
- [ambiguity-testability] §6.3 battery check 1 (Subsumption) has no mechanical pass condition — refuted: §16 discloses the grouping as a model judgment with the dissolve question as the gate, so the spec explicitly accepts it.
- [ambiguity-testability] §7.2 Phase 4's "union of the rows matched by every component" leaves `Category: Composite` matching no row in fix-auto's Step 4.1 table — refuted: "plus the rows matched by the composite's own change" covers the change-keyed rows, and §4.1 bars OWASP/CWE on a composite by design.
- [ambiguity-testability] §7.2 Phase 6 would grade all-resolved-but-verification-failed as Fixed — refuted: the "Fixed" clause requires "and verification passed", and the next clause catches the case as Partially Fixed.
- [ambiguity-testability] §7.3's table has no column for `unresolved (no location)` or `skipped (rejected)` — refuted: both are non-`resolved`, the `unresolved` column's "no status" applies, and a skipped component already carries `🚫 Rejected`.

### completeness
- [completeness] Ordering/conflict between findings that touch the same code but share no cause — §2 names it a non-goal.
- [completeness] Cross-report-file grouping (a cause shared by a review and a QA finding) — §2 non-goal and §16's last residual risk.
- [completeness] No machine enforcement of the fixer's root-cause-only rule — §2 non-goal and §16's second residual risk.
- [completeness] `/qa:loop` integration — §2 non-goal, and §10 states the untouched contract explicitly.
- [completeness] No batching bound for a file with many candidates — §6.3 states 'one call per source file' explicitly; the pass is deliberately unbatched and §16 records the cost.
- [completeness] §7.3 has no column for a component whose Result is `unresolved (no location)` — §7.2's Result enum plus the Fixed/Partially-Fixed rule place it with `unresolved`.
- [completeness] §6.4 does not validate the agent's `Effort` value — format detail, not a load-bearing decision.
- [completeness] The `Source` column cell for a composite row in `/fix-all`'s pre-flight — Step 2.4's existing rule already yields `—` for a block with no `Source:` field; the substantive half (whether provenance should be inherited at all) is reported separately.
- [completeness] §5.2 step 1 does not handle a basis title matching two findings — low blast radius, and §5.1's `DOC-NNN` route plus the challenger-survivor set make it marginal.
- [completeness] A dissolution of a persisted composite is lost when `/fix-report` selection ends with nothing selected — §6.8's 'An aborted run writes nothing' covers a stopped run, and the composite is offered again next run.
- [completeness] A component block edited between decision and dispatch is not covered by the composite's `Decision-pin` block hash — §7.1 excludes fixed/rejected components from the payload and §12 records the `/fix`-alone case, so the degeneracy rule catches it.
- [completeness] COMP ID reuse after a dissolved or unselected proposal — §6.4's ID-assignment paragraph states the release-and-reassign rule outright.
- [completeness] `/fix-all` single-file mode pointed at a report outside `docs/reviews/` — §6.1's directory rule settles it (no pass).
- [completeness] Where `Part-of` goes on a block with no `**ID:**` line — §4.2 states 'directly after the heading where the block has no `**ID:**` line'.
- [completeness] Composites nesting inside `Composed-of` — §4.3 and §12 both close it.
- [completeness] Grouping is a model judgment / a wrong group costs one structural change — §16's first residual risk.
- [completeness] A composite's `Fixed` is only as hard as its components' tools select — §16's fourth residual risk.
- [completeness] Cross-verifier escalating severity above the members' maximum — §4.3 permits it for review-origin and fixes the pass at exactly the maximum.
- [completeness] Hand-edited composite with severity below a component's — §12's closed list handles it.
- [completeness] Whether a needs-decision composite reaches `/fix-all` Step 5 as one entry — §8's opening sentence plus §6.2's removal of bound components settle it.

### feasibility
- [feasibility] §4.3's claim that `check-prefix-sync.sh` requires three consumers to carry `COMP` — verified true: the script checks fix.md's alternation regex, fix-auto.md's Category enum against the doc table's Category column, and extract-issue-ids.sh's PREFIX_RE against canonical-minus-QA; a `| Composite | COMP | docs/reviews/ |` row parses under its awk and the `>= 5` floor holds.
- [feasibility] New step numbers 1.6 / 2.4.5 / 3.0 / 2.1.5 / 2.3.5 / 0.4.5 colliding with existing steps — checked every one: none collide and each anchor step exists.
- [feasibility] §6.1's claim that Step 1.6 follows Steps 1.3/1.4/1.5 and precedes the severity floor — all three exist in both commands, and fix-all's floor is Step 2.2; `/fix-report` has no floor at all, but the placement is still fully determined by "after Step 1.5".
- [feasibility] §13.3's claim that `tools: Read, Grep, Glob` passes `check_agent_frontmatter.py` — verified: all three are in CANONICAL_TOOLS, none stripped or background-lost, all four keys permitted.
- [feasibility] §13.3's claim that the fix commands gain no `Bash(...)` grants so `check_execution_boundary.py` is unaffected — verified against the grant registry and both commands' `allowed-tools:`.
- [feasibility] AskUserQuestion's 4-option ceiling breaking §6.7's dissolve question or §9's three-option question — §6.7 already pages at four per call with no appended item, and `fix-report.md` Step 2.2b plus Step 2.3 confirm both the ceiling and that an empty multiSelect answer is a supported shape.
- [feasibility] `/fix`'s `allowed-tools:` lacking `AskUserQuestion` for §9's Step 0.4.5 question — CLAUDE.md states `allowed-tools` in command frontmatter pre-approves without affecting availability, and `/fix` already asks the user in prose at Phase 1 and Phase 3.
- [feasibility] §4.3's claim that `Composed-of`/`Part-of`/`Origin` are hashed into `**Decision-pin:**` and travel in the dispatch copy with the closed exclusion list unchanged — verified: the exclusion list names eight loop-written fields and none of the three, and §6.8's write-before-any-pin ordering holds in both commands.
- [feasibility] §8's `:ref` pin clause being incompatible with the skill — verified compatible: roles are an alternation, the pre-dispatch comparison covers both, and stage 4's expected set is `:edit` alone.
- [feasibility] §5.1's claim that bases must be cited by title because only the documentation auditor emits IDs — verified: review.md Step 5.6 assigns all IDs after the cross-verifier has returned.
- [feasibility] §5.2's ordering claim (resolve bases against findings that survived the challenger) — verified: review.md Step 5.5 item 5 applies challenger decisions at sub-item 1 and adds composite findings at sub-item 2.
- [feasibility] §6.3's `tool: …` citation form being invented — verified present in `decision-analyst.md`'s `Findings` row.
- [feasibility] §6.3's untrusted-wrap reuse requiring a nonce the fix commands cannot generate — refuted as pre-existing: decision-gate stage 1 already requires the same generation today.
- [feasibility] §6.3's "files are dispatched in parallel" being unreachable without `TaskOutput` — refuted: concurrent `Task` calls in one turn achieve it, and at most one review file is ever eligible.
- [feasibility] The severity floor admitting a composite while it would have dropped a low-severity bound component — settled by §2's goal and §6.2's removal of bound components from the individual list.
- [feasibility] §6.8's `Edit`-based insertion before its first component's heading failing uniqueness — refuted: headings carry unique `PREFIX-NNN` IDs and the recipe mirrors Step 4.1's pattern.
- [feasibility] `Part-of` breaking Step 4.1.5's positional "next non-blank line is `**Status:**`" check — refuted: §4.2 places it after the `**ID:**` line, and the insert-after-heading recipe always writes a later status above it.
- [feasibility] `Part-of`/`Composed-of` being swallowed by `fix-auto`'s Remediation capture — refuted: both sit above `**Problem:**`, not appended at the end of the block.
- [feasibility] §7.1 vs §7.2 on excluded/rejected components and missing blocks — a real defect, but internal consistency, outside this lens's mandate.
- [feasibility] §6.7's "One `AskUserQuestion`" contradicting its own paging — internal consistency, outside this lens's mandate; the paging itself is feasible.
- [feasibility] §8's multi-block payload conflicting with `decision-analyst.md`'s "exactly one finding block" input contract — refuted: §17 lists both `decision-analyst.md` and `decision-gate/SKILL.md` as changed files.
- [feasibility] §13.2's claim that `check-prefix-sync.sh` today runs only by hand — verified: none of the five workflows invokes it.

### doctrine-compliance
- [doctrine-compliance] Item 1 (name the oracle, state what it cannot verify) — met: §7.2 Phase 4 names it and §16 states the limit.
- [doctrine-compliance] Item 3 (disclose, don't gate, on coverage) — met: the Phase 6 Components table carries `unresolved (no location)` as its own Result value, §11's row shows per-component markers, and the shortfall degrades to a released component rather than a silent pass.
- [doctrine-compliance] Item 7 (stop on no-progress; stopped ≠ success) — met well enough: a Failed composite is reported `❌ Failed`, a `⚠️ Partially Fixed` composite closes terminally, a needs-decision composite inherits the gate's two-attempt retirement, and §6.7 re-offers a still-open composite for dissolution on every later run.
- [doctrine-compliance] Item 8 (document the residual-risk list) — met: §16 enumerates five residuals; the items it omits are reported under their own bar numbers.
- [doctrine-compliance] Item 11 (writes scoped and recoverable) — met: §16 "Every fix stays uncommitted"; §6.8's writes are heading-anchored `Edit`s each verified by re-read, and "An aborted run writes nothing".
- [doctrine-compliance] Item 10 (input hash-pinning against mid-run tampering) — no pin covers an auto composite's blocks between Step 1.6 and Step 3.0, but §6.8's `old_string` anchoring fails closed on a changed heading and §4.3 puts the new fields inside the `**Decision-pin:**` hash on the gated path; the exposure is the pre-existing auto path's.
- [doctrine-compliance] Item 2 (broader claim: the pass trusts the analyst) — refuted by §6.4, which recomputes `Severity`, re-checks membership, count and disjointness, and drops any failing proposal.
- [doctrine-compliance] Item 5 (untrusted-input framing reinvented) — refuted: §6.3 reuses `decision-gate` stage 1's nonce-bound wrapping by reference.
- [doctrine-compliance] Item 2 (verifier handed its own checks) — refuted: §7.1 routes every dispatched block through stage 3's dispatch-copy rule, so `**Verification-plan:**` is stripped.
- [doctrine-compliance] §7.3 has no row for `unresolved (no location)` or `skipped (rejected)` — arbitrable and an internal-consistency matter rather than a bar item.
- [doctrine-compliance] Item 9 (grouping assertion wholly unguarded) — refuted in part: §6.3 imposes the `finding-falsification` battery with five named checks and mandatory tool citations, and §5.1 keeps the challenger provenance for review-origin composites; only the gate's render survives, reported as SR-024.

### ux
- [ux] `/fix-report` asks the dissolve question before the checklist, spending an answer on composites the user may never select — §6.9 states the rationale.
- [ux] `/fix-all` asks the dissolve question before the confirmation gate, so an aborted run has spent an answer — §6.7 orders it deliberately so the gate's count is post-dissolve, and §6.8 keeps the abort write-free.
- [ux] Persisted review-origin composites are re-offered for dissolution on every run until fixed — this is the recorded user decision.
- [ux] The question copy never explains what dissolving costs the user — the question text says "into their components" and the released components then appear in the delta line, the gate count and the checklist.
- [ux] A `multiSelect` question may not permit submitting zero selections — platform capability, outside this lens; §6.7 states the intent in the question text.
- [ux] No dissolve question is defined for the pass-unavailable case — §6.5 yields zero proposals, so the §6.7 trigger simply does not fire.
- [ux] `/fix-all` Step 2.5's option label `Yes — fix all <total_count>` could go stale after a dissolve — §6.7 says the gate uses the new count (covered instead by the delta-line finding).
- [ux] Bound components disappear from the §6.6 pre-flight issue table — §6.6 states this deliberately and the Composites block lists them; the identifiability gap is reported against the dissolve question's copy instead.
- [ux] A Fix Summary row for a composite with many components could overflow the table cell — the existing 60-char title truncation is inherited and §11 does not change it.
- [ux] `/fix COMP-001` on a degenerate composite stops without offering to fix the lone component — §9 requires the message to name the remaining component as the thing to fix.
- [ux] No consent question before fix-time markers are written into the user's report — §6.8 places persistence after the confirmation gate in `/fix-all` and after selection in `/fix-report`, which is the consent.
- [ux] §11's `Dropped by validation: SEC-004 + SEC-005` line reports a grouping the user never saw — it is post-hoc disclosure per §6.4, not a question, and costs no answers.

### contracts
- [contracts] Adding `Composite | COMP | docs/reviews/` to the canonical table and COMP/`Composite` to the three consumers — verified against `check-prefix-sync.sh`: no CI break.
- [contracts] `extract-issue-ids.sh` would miss composites — refuted: its heading regex matches `### [HIGH] COMP-001: …` unchanged once COMP is in `PREFIX_RE`.
- [contracts] `/fix COMP-001` routing needs a new case arm — refuted: `fix.md` Step 0.1's default arm already routes COMP.
- [contracts] The three new fields break `fix-auto` Phase 1 — refuted: Phase 1's field table is a lookup of named fields, not a closed parse; unknown lines are ignored.
- [contracts] The new fields break the `**Decision-pin:**` closed exclusion list — refuted: that list is closed over loop-written lines only; the three are authored and are hashed like any authored line.
- [contracts] `**Part-of:**` written "directly after the heading" would displace the slot-order invariant — refuted: a candidate carries no `**Status:**` line, and the insert-after-heading recipe always writes a later status above the Part-of line.
- [contracts] `review.md`'s "Review Comment Format" Category enum is not updated with `Composite` — refuted: that enum is already stale (omits `Testing`) and `check-prefix-sync.sh` deliberately does not read it.
- [contracts] `fix-auto` Phase 4.1 has no `Category = Composite` row — refuted: §7.2 sources tools from the union of component rows plus the composite's own change.
- [contracts] Model-authored composite text persisted into the report could contain `---` or `### ` and truncate the block — refuted as not introduced here: `/review` already writes auditor-authored text into blocks read by the same rule.
- [contracts] §6.2 writes a bare `✅ Fixed` without the `(YYYY-MM-DD)` the grammar requires — refuted: §11 routes every composite status through Step 4.1's recipe.
- [contracts] The qa plugin's shared block format needs updating, so "`/qa:loop` is untouched" is false — refuted: `plugins/qa/skills/report-format/SKILL.md` fixes `Category: Testing` and the `QA` prefix only.
- [contracts] A composite at max severity drags sub-floor components into a floored run — refuted: §4.3 states the invariant in the only direction that could silently drop a finding, and fixing a component below the floor as part of one root-cause change is the feature's stated point.
- [contracts] COMP ID reuse after a dissolved proposal could collide — refuted: §6.4 states the release explicitly, and a number never written collides with nothing on disk.

### fix-coherence
- None — the verifier was never dispatched.

## Residual risks

- One panel pass; the tightened major anchor; stochastic panel and verifier; no token ceiling; majors carry no challenger; no mid-run resume and in-context state across the gate; growth disclosed not limited; interaction cost disclosed not budgeted; doctrine item 4 not met by this pipeline.
- The budget ceiling, observed here on the time axis rather than the dispatch axis the command documents: with the full seven-lens roster the panel alone consumed 559 s of the 900 s default, and orchestration over seven returns plus one challenger exhausted the rest before batch A. A spec of this size with a full panel needs `--time-budget` of roughly 2400–3600 s to reach batch A and the verifier. The documented dispatch ceiling (seven or more criticals stop before batch A) did not bind — one critical only.
- Snapshots are per-run, uncommitted and never pruned — recovery depends on picking the right `run<N>`, and a `git clean` of an untracked `reviews/` directory takes all of them.
- The one decision collected (SR-022 → B) is recorded above only; a re-run asks it afresh.

## Recovery

- Loop-touched files: this report only. The spec was not modified (pre-loop and post-loop hashes are equal).
- Snapshot: `docs/superpowers/specs/reviews/2026-09-14-composite-findings-design.pre-loop.run1.bak`.
- Never `git restore` on the spec; the snapshot is the recovery path. Nothing was committed.
